{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall do My ordinances</b> — these are the matters stated in the Torah that, had they not been stated, reason would have dictated them; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 18:4). And in Torat Kohanim (chapter 13, halakhah 9): these are the matters written in the Torah that, had they not been written, justice would have required writing them, such as robbery, forbidden sexual relations, idolatry, bloodshed, and blaspheming the Name. And according to the plain sense, \"My ordinances\" means what it sounds like: the civil laws stated in the parashah of \"And these are the ordinances\" and throughout the Torah. Therefore it says, \"which a person shall do and live by them,\" for the laws were given for the life of humanity in the settlement of lands and the well-being of the individual, so that one person will not harm another and will not kill him. Thus Ezekiel mentioned many times regarding the ordinances: \"which a person shall do and live by them\" (Ezekiel 20:11, 13, 21), and regarding the Sabbaths he says (ibid. verse 12), \"to be a sign between Me and them,\" and similarly in Nehemiah (9:29), \"and in Your ordinances they sinned against them, which a person shall do and live by them.\" And our Rabbis said (Yoma 85b), \"and live by them\" — and not die because of them, to teach that the preservation of life overrides the Sabbath and the commandments. And its midrash (Torat Kohanim, parashah 9, halakhah 10): \"and live by them\" — in the World to Come; should you say in this world, is he not ultimately destined to die?\n\nIf so, the phrase \"which a person shall do\" also recurs in application to \"My statutes.\" And know that the life of a person through the commandments is according to his preparation for them. For one who performs the commandments not for their own sake, with the intention of receiving reward, will live by them in this world many days, with wealth, possessions, and honor. Regarding this it is stated (Proverbs 3:16), \"In her left hand are wealth and honor,\" and they explained (Shabbat 63a): for those who tarry with her in order to receive reward, wealth and honor. Likewise, those who engage in the commandments with the intention of meriting through them the World to Come — they are the ones who serve out of fear — they merit through their intention to be delivered from the judgment of the wicked, and their souls depend on the Good. And those who engage in the commandments out of love, as justice and as is fitting, together with their involvement in this world — in the manner mentioned in the Torah in the parashah of \"If in My statutes\" (Leviticus 26:5), \"your threshing shall overtake the vintage,\" and so on — they will merit in this world a good life according to the way of the world, and their merit for the life of the World to Come is complete there. And those who abandon all the affairs of this world and pay no attention to it, as though they have no body, and all their thought and intention are upon their Creator alone — as was the case with Elijah — their souls cleaving to the honored Name, they will live forever in body and in soul, as is evident in Scripture regarding Elijah, and as is known from him through the Kabbalah, and as is recounted in the midrashim regarding Enoch and the dwellers of the World to Come who stand in the resurrection of the dead. Therefore the verses state regarding the reward of the commandments: \"so that your days may be long\" (Exodus 20:12), \"so that you may live\" (Deuteronomy 16:20), \"and you shall prolong your days\" (ibid. 22:7), for the expression encompasses all types of life as is fitting for each person."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:20.349841645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}